## Why This Is Asked
This question explores the challenge of acronym expansion and text normalization in a multilingual, on-device environment with stringent latency, memory, and privacy requirements.

## Key Concepts
- On-device NLP with privacy-preserving inference
- Lightweight hybrid approach combining rule-based and ML methods
- Multilingual text normalization and tokenization strategies
- End-to-end latency budgeting and performance evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch: dictionary lookup for abbreviations
const abbreviationDict = {
  'eod': 'end of day',
  'fyi': 'for your information',
  'ceo': 'chief executive officer'
};

function normalizeText(text, lang = 'en') {
  // Tokenize and expand abbreviations
  const tokens = text.toLowerCase().split(/\s+/);
  const normalized = tokens.map(token => 
    abbreviationDict[token] || token
  );
  return normalized.join(' ');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Examines on-device learning, privacy-preserving federated updates, drift handling, and governance in a multilingual streaming setting, critical for scalable, privacy-conscious NLP systems.

## Key Concepts

- On-device inference and 8-bit quantization
- Language adapters and adapter-based fine-tuning
- Federated learning with secure aggregation and differential privacy
- Drift detection across languages and streams
- Canary rollouts and rollback governance

## Code Example

```javascript
// Federated averaging sketch (simplified)
function aggregate(updates) {
  let totalN = updates.reduce((a, u) => a + u.n, 0);
  const dim = updates[0].weights.length;
  const sum = new Array(dim).fill(0);
  for (const u of updates) {
    for (let i = 0; i < dim; i++) sum[i] += u.weights[i] * u.n;
  }
  return sum.map(v => v / totalN);
}
```

